{
  "id": "a-rainy-day",
  "title": "A Rainy Day",
  "level": "A1",
  "summary": "Mia wants to play outside, but it is raining. What can she do until the sun comes back?",
  "teachingMethod": "Story-based",
  "teachingMethodReason": "Meets the course's criteria for story-based instruction: a formulaic opening (\"This is Mia...\", the same opening the-red-ball uses), a clear problem (the rain stops her playing), a short series of events while she waits, and a resolution (the sun returns and she runs out). It is not TBL - the reader never does a task or reaches an outcome (Willis 1996/Nunan 2006) - and not CLIL, since weather here is a setting rather than content being taught. Chosen to carry the present continuous, which no other A1 book in the library uses: rain that is happening right now is the most natural context for it.",
  "grammarFeatures": [
    "Present continuous for what is happening now (is raining, is looking)",
    "\"To be\" statements (Mia is sad)",
    "Simple present for wants and likes (she wants to play)",
    "Basic adjectives (sad, happy, wet)"
  ],
  "introFunFacts": [
    "🌧️ Everything in this story is happening RIGHT NOW - look for words ending in '-ing'!",
    "😀 Watch Mia's feelings change from the first page to the last one.",
    "🐱 The cat does not mind the rain at all. See if you can spot why!"
  ],
